{
  "image_name": "1dcb257966a45f2b656b02cd57a1332b.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares the genome organization and membrane-associated protein machinery of two related flaviviral groups, labeled Flavivirus and Hepacivirus. In both, a single long viral polyprotein is shown being cut into structural proteins at the left and non-structural proteins at the right, with untranslated regions at both ends and cleavage events marked along the chain.\n\nThe main message is that these viruses share a broadly similar strategy—translation of one polyprotein followed by proteolytic processing and assembly of replication complexes on intracellular membranes—but differ in the exact proteins and their roles. The lower schematics place the mature proteins in a membrane, highlighting envelope proteins, small transmembrane cofactors, and large enzymatic proteins.\n\nFor Flavivirus, the structural region includes capsid, prM, and E, while the non-structural proteins include NS1 through NS5. NS3 is depicted as a protease/helicase working with NS2B, NS4A and NS4B are tied to membrane reorganization, and NS5 carries methyltransferase and polymerase functions for RNA synthesis. For Hepacivirus, the proteins are C, E1, E2, p7, NS2, NS3, NS4A/B, NS5A, and NS5B. Here, p7 appears as an ion channel, NS3-NS4A forms the protease/helicase complex, NS4B reorganizes membranes, NS5A supports phosphoprotein RNA replication assembly, and NS5B is the polymerase.",
